# Install as .github/workflows/precedent-check.yml in a PRACTICE SET -- an
# individual or team source set that authors its own practices/ and vendors
# the engine into its own tools/. See GITHUB_ACTIONS.md, "Install in a
# Practice-Set Repository". Replaces views-drift.yml.template, which no
# longer exists as a separate file (folded in below as this workflow's own
# job).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (practice: cite-the-incident). Every source set had the
# same hole: the only workflows any of them carried called a SINGLE check's
# function directly -- written that way precisely because precedent_check.py
# skipped that check in a source set -- and nothing ran the suite. So a set
# was gated on one or two rules it had hand-wired, and silent on the rest of
# its own catalogue.
#
# binds_publishers (BestPractice PR #261, 2026-09-12) removed the reason
# those hand-wired workflows existed: a check whose subject is the practice a
# repo PUBLISHES now runs in the repo publishing it. That makes the suite
# worth running here, and this workflow is the thing that runs it.
#
# NOT --strict, on purpose. Most registered checks belong to practices a
# source set does not resolve, and they skip by design -- measured in a
# freshly bootstrapped set on 2026-09-13, 41 of them. --strict turns every
# one of those into a failure, which leaves the gate permanently red and
# teaches everyone to ignore it; a gate nobody reads is worse than no gate.
# Violations, errors and a refusal below each fail the run on their own.
#
# WHY THE views-drift JOB EXISTS, NOT JUST precedent-check (practice:
# cite-the-incident). Every set that vendors the engine generates AGENTS.md's
# loader block, MAP.md and GLOSSARY.md, and until 2026-09-11 nothing checked
# any of them anywhere but in Precedent's own repo (deep-check.yml runs
# verify_harness.py, deliberately not vendored into a source set). A real
# individual set's MAP.md sat three practices stale under a generated header
# claiming a guard was failing the build on exactly that. precedent_check.py's
# own generated-artifact-provenance check (binds a source set since
# BestPractice PR #261) covers MAP.md/GLOSSARY.md via the same
# build_views.py --check subprocess the views-drift job below runs, but NOT
# AGENTS.md's generated loader block -- a hand-authored file with one
# generated section, a different shape only verify_harness.py used to catch.
# The views-drift job is the only thing in a source set that catches
# loader-block drift; that is why it stays its own job with its own failure
# message rather than folding into precedent-check's registry of checks.
#
# ONE WORKFLOW, NOT TWO (2026-09-19, practice: cite-the-incident). This file
# and views-drift.yml.template used to be separate, each its own job with
# its own debounce STEP. A debounce step only skips the steps AFTER it -- the
# job itself still starts a runner and checks out the tree to reach that
# decision, and GitHub bills a job for at least one whole minute no matter
# how quickly it finishes. Two workflows meant two billed minutes on every
# push regardless of what the debounce window decided. Measured against a
# real account's usage report, on four practice sets installed from this
# template: push-triggered workflows billed 142 of 165 Actions minutes in
# one morning, almost entirely pushes the debounce window would have
# skipped, split across two jobs that each paid their own one-minute floor
# to say so.
#
# The fix is not a cheaper debounce decision -- billing rounds up to a whole
# minute regardless of how many seconds a job actually runs, so a faster
# checkout does not lower the bill. The fix is fewer jobs that
# unconditionally start. This file makes the debounce decision exactly ONCE,
# in its own `debounce` job below, and the two checks are separate jobs
# gated on that job's output via `needs:` + `if:` rather than steps inside a
# job that already started. A job whose `if:` evaluates false is reported
# SKIPPED and never allocates a runner -- it is not billed at all. On the
# common (skip) push this workflow bills one job-minute instead of two; on
# the rare push that actually runs the suite it bills about what it always
# did (the debounce job's own minute, plus each check's own run, still in
# parallel).
#
# The full incident, measurements, and the account that hit this are
# recorded in this repo's own spec/CI_MINUTES_PLAN.md, item 8. That's
# history worth reading if you're deciding whether to deviate from this
# template's shape; it is not required reading to install it as shipped.

name: Precedent checks

on:
  # pull_request (opened, synchronize) PLUS push scoped to the branch(es)
  # that actually receive merges -- `main` below is the default a fresh
  # install gets. CUSTOMIZE THE BRANCH LIST if this repo's own routine merge
  # target is not just `main`: Precedent's own equivalent workflows in this
  # repo list `[main, precedent-beta-v01]`, because routine work here lands
  # on precedent-beta-v01, not main (see GITHUB_ACTIONS.md). Whatever
  # branch(es) a session actually merges into in the repo this installs
  # into, list them here.
  #
  # WHY BOTH, NOT PUSH ALONE (2026-09-19, the second of two same-day
  # changes -- item 8 above has the full account). `push:`, unrestricted to
  # any branch, was this template's design from 2026-09-14 to 2026-09-19: a
  # session pushing straight to a source set's own branch -- the normal way
  # work lands in a private, single-owner practice set -- otherwise ran no
  # check at all. Restricting push to named branches alone (tried first,
  # same day) reopened that exact gap for every other branch, and a
  # different repo's CI caught a real error on an open PR the same day --
  # exactly the case branch-restriction-alone gives up on. `pull_request:`
  # restores it at close to zero added cost: a branch with NO open PR
  # triggers nothing here at all -- GitHub does not even evaluate this
  # workflow, cheaper than debounce ever was. A branch WITH an open PR gets
  # checked, debounced the same way repeated pushes to it already are.
  #
  # WHY THIS DOESN'T DOUBLE-BILL, which is the reason earlier versions of
  # this template ran push alone: a push run and a pull_request run test
  # the identical tree whenever a push lands on a branch that ALSO matches
  # push's own filter -- doc-lint.yml.template measured this directly, 235
  # runs in matched pairs before it was fixed the same way this file now is.
  # That collision needs push to be listening on the SAME branch
  # pull_request is watching. With push scoped to only the branch(es) named
  # below -- none of which is ever a PR's own head branch -- a PR's branch
  # only ever gets pull_request events, and a named branch only ever gets
  # push events (a PR's merge commit landing on main is a push to main, not
  # a synchronize on the now-closed PR branch). Nothing here fires twice for
  # the same commit.
  pull_request:
    types: [opened, synchronize]
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

# No `paths:` filter, for the same reason each check job below gives on its
# own terms: the inputs to these checks are wider than they look -- a
# practice's frontmatter, the engine itself, a check script a practice
# names in checked_by, the routing vocabulary, precedent.json's visibility.
# A filter that misses one input is a workflow that reports nothing while
# looking green.

# CONCURRENCY, so a burst of pushes to the same branch does not pay for
# every run in the burst (2026-09-16). A second run starting on this branch
# cancels whichever earlier run for it is still going, rather than billing
# both to completion. One group for the whole workflow -- all three jobs
# below rise and fall together.
concurrency:
  group: precedent-check-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

# Read-only on purpose: this reports, and has no business holding a token
# that could write. actions: read is for the debounce job below, which
# lists this workflow's own past runs -- still no write access anywhere.
permissions:
  contents: read
  actions: read

# RUNNER, OVERRIDABLE PER REPO (2026-09-20). All three jobs below run on
# `ubuntu-latest` unless this repo declares a `PRECEDENT_RUNNER` repository
# variable (Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables) --
# e.g. `self-hosted`, for an adopter who already operates their own runner.
# Left unset, nothing here changes. This is an adopter's own opt-in, not
# something Precedent can provide for them: a self-hosted runner means
# their code executing on infrastructure they control, which nobody else
# can stand up on their behalf. spec/CI_MINUTES_PLAN.md item 6 has the
# trade-offs; GITHUB_ACTIONS.md's "Controlling Actions Minutes" is where
# this variable is documented alongside the levers that need no opt-in at
# all (ci_workflows disabled by default, the debounce job, branch scoping).
jobs:
  # ONE job, so the "have we looked recently" decision is billed once. Every
  # job below this one is gated with `needs: debounce` + `if:`, never a step
  # inside a job that already started -- see the header comment for why
  # that distinction is the entire point of this file's shape.
  debounce:
    name: Decide whether this push needs a fresh check
    runs-on: ${{ vars.PRECEDENT_RUNNER || 'ubuntu-latest' }}
    outputs:
      skip: ${{ steps.debounce.outputs.skip }}
    steps:
      # No fetch-depth override here -- this job only reads identity.json
      # out of the working tree, never git history, so the default shallow
      # checkout is enough. Each check below takes its own full checkout
      # when it actually runs.
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      # DEBOUNCE (2026-09-16, moved into its own job 2026-09-19, window
      # tightened 360 -> 30 minutes the same day as the branches:[main]
      # restriction above, widened to 720 on 2026-09-20 -- item 11): a set
      # pushed to constantly pays for the whole suite on every push even
      # with nothing ever overlapping enough for concurrency above to
      # cancel it. Reads `ci_debounce_minutes` from this set's own
      # identity.json (default 720 minutes = 12 hours; 0 disables it; a
      # TEAM set has no identity.json and always gets the default -- a
      # known limit, not an oversight) and skips both checks below when the
      # last COMPLETED run of this workflow on this branch finished inside
      # that window. spec/CI_MINUTES_PLAN.md's item 4: this is the
      # whole-catalogue advisory check, not a security backstop. **This
      # reopens the exact staleness item 8 narrowed the window to avoid**:
      # main can now sit unchecked for up to 12 hours after a push, not 30
      # minutes -- item 11 records that trade as accepted, explicitly, not
      # overlooked. A repo that wants the tighter window back sets
      # `ci_debounce_minutes` itself in its own identity.json/precedent.json
      # rather than waiting on this template's default to change again.
      - name: Skip if checked recently (debounce)
        id: debounce
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
        run: |
          set -eu
          minutes="$(python3 - <<'PY'
          import json, pathlib
          try:
              d = json.loads(pathlib.Path('identity.json').read_text())
          except (OSError, ValueError):
              d = {}
          try:
              v = int(d.get('ci_debounce_minutes', 720))
          except (TypeError, ValueError):
              v = 720
          print(v)
          PY
          )"
          if [ "$minutes" -le 0 ]; then
            echo "ci_debounce_minutes: $minutes -- debounce off, running"
            echo "skip=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
            exit 0
          fi
          last="$(gh run list --workflow "${{ github.workflow }}" \
                   --branch "${{ github.ref_name }}" --status completed \
                   --limit 1 --json updatedAt --jq '.[0].updatedAt // empty' \
                   2>/dev/null || true)"
          if [ -z "$last" ]; then
            echo "no prior completed run on this branch -- running"
            echo "skip=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
            exit 0
          fi
          elapsed=$(( ($(date -u +%s) - $(date -u -d "$last" +%s)) / 60 ))
          if [ "$elapsed" -lt "$minutes" ]; then
            echo "last completed run was ${elapsed}m ago, inside the " \
                 "${minutes}m window -- skipping"
            echo "skip=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          else
            echo "last completed run was ${elapsed}m ago, outside the " \
                 "${minutes}m window -- running"
            echo "skip=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          fi

  precedent-check:
    name: precedent_check.py (whole suite)
    needs: debounce
    if: needs.debounce.outputs.skip != 'true'
    runs-on: ${{ vars.PRECEDENT_RUNNER || 'ubuntu-latest' }}

    steps:
      # The PR HEAD, not refs/pull/N/merge -- auditing the merge tree reports
      # findings belonging to neither branch, which the author cannot act on.
      - name: Check out the pull request head
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}
          # LOAD-BEARING, and the failure it prevents is silent. Several
          # checks are scope 'tree' and walk `git log` over the repo's real
          # history. On the actions/checkout default (depth 1) that history
          # is not there, and those checks report SKIPPED with a reason
          # rather than running -- a skip does not fail the run, so a shallow
          # checkout quietly shrinks coverage while the job stays green.
          #
          # MEASURED, not argued, 2026-09-13 in a real practice set: same
          # tree, same commit, a shallow checkout reported 13 passed/42
          # skipped and a full one 14 passed/41 skipped. Exactly one check
          # moved -- a scope 'tree' check that walks git log -- and it moved
          # from SKIPPED to running, with the job green either way. One check
          # is what this line is worth, and you cannot see it go.
          #
          # BestPractice's own deep-check.yml carries this same line after
          # exactly that bug ran undetected on every push for days.
          fetch-depth: 0

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"

      # The vendored ENGINE needs no third-party module -- but a check script
      # this set names in a practice's `checked_by:` may, and PyYAML is the
      # common one. Those scripts run as subprocesses under a documented exit
      # contract (0 clean, 1 violated, 2 could-not-run), and an uncaught
      # ModuleNotFoundError exits 1 -- so a missing dependency is reported as
      # a VIOLATION with its traceback, not as a skip. That is the loud
      # failure and the right one, but only if you can tell it from a real
      # finding; installing the module here is cheaper than reading a
      # traceback on a green-looking day. Unpinned, matching BestPractice's
      # own deep-check.yml and docs.yml.
      - name: Install PyYAML
        run: python -m pip install pyyaml

      # FOUR NAMED STEPS, not one. GitHub Actions shows step NAMES on a run's
      # summary page and log bodies only when you open them, so a single
      # combined step renders every failure as one red X saying "Run the
      # Precedent checks" -- which is least legible for the refusals, and a
      # refusal fires on somebody who has not read this file.

      - name: Refuse if this repo is not a source set carrying the engine
        run: |
          set -eu
          if [ ! -f tools/precedent_check.py ]; then
            if [ -f process/upstream/tools/precedent_check.py ]; then
              echo "This repo vendors the engine under process/upstream/, which is" \
                   "INSTALL.md section 1's CONSUMING-repo layout, not a practice set." \
                   "Its practices/ is materialized from sources a CI checkout does not" \
                   "have, so a run here would report coverage it does not possess." \
                   "Remove this workflow; GITHUB_ACTIONS.md's Limits section says what" \
                   "does cover a consuming repo." >&2
              exit 1
            fi
            echo "No tools/precedent_check.py -- the engine was never vendored into" \
                 "this repo, or it lives somewhere this workflow does not know about." \
                 "Vendor it (tools/precedent_vendor_engine.py) or add the path here." >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          # A practices/ tree is what a publisher-bound check binds. Without it
          # the suite still runs and still reports PASSES -- measured on this
          # engine with practices/ removed: `3 passed, 0 violated`, from checks
          # that test the engine rather than a catalogue. So the zero-coverage
          # backstop below does NOT catch this: it is not zero, it is three, and
          # a green tick would mean the publisher checks had nothing to look at.
          if [ ! -d practices ]; then
            echo "No practices/ directory -- this repo publishes no practice files," \
                 "so the checks that bind a publisher have nothing to bind. The run" \
                 "would still report passes from engine-level checks, which is why" \
                 "this is refused here rather than left to the backstop. Remove this" \
                 "workflow, or vendor the catalogue this repo is meant to publish." >&2
            exit 1
          fi

      - name: Refuse if the vendored engine predates binds_publishers
        run: |
          set -eu
          # INSPECT THE REGISTRY, NEVER GREP FOR THE NAME. An earlier version of
          # this workflow ran `grep -q 'binds_publishers' tools/precedent_check.py`,
          # and that passes on any engine that merely MENTIONS the string -- a
          # comment, a docstring, or the text of a refusal about the flag it just
          # dropped. Measured on this engine: with the flag stripped from all
          # three registrations and only prose left behind, the grep still
          # reported PASSES while the registry reported zero flagged checks. The
          # engine's own module docstring discusses binds_publishers, so the grep
          # form was already satisfied by prose alone.
          #
          # Importing also refuses an engine that cannot be imported, naming the
          # exception here instead of failing confusingly at the run step.
          python3 - <<'PY'
          import sys
          sys.path.insert(0, 'tools')
          try:
              import precedent_check as pc
          except Exception as e:
              sys.exit(f'REFUSING: tools/precedent_check.py did not import ({e!r}), '
                       f'so what it checks cannot be established.')
          flagged = sorted(s for s, c in pc.CHECKS.items() if c.get('binds_publishers'))
          if not flagged:
              sys.exit(
                  'REFUSING: no registered check carries binds_publishers, so this '
                  'engine predates BestPractice PR #261 (2026-09-12) or has lost the '
                  'flag. In a practice set such an engine skips the checks whose '
                  'practice lives upstream and still exits 0 -- measured 5 passed/44 '
                  'skipped against 8 passed/41 skipped on the same tree -- so this '
                  'workflow would be quietly green while missing exactly the checks '
                  'that bind what this repo publishes. Refresh the engine: '
                  'python3 tools/precedent_vendor_engine.py refresh')
          print(f'engine carries binds_publishers on {len(flagged)} check(s): '
                + ', '.join(flagged))
          PY

      - name: Run every check that binds this repo
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          # NOT --strict -- see the header. Most registered checks belong to
          # levels a source set does not resolve and skip by design; --strict
          # makes each a failure and leaves the gate permanently red.
          #
          # tee, because the next step reads this output. A violation exits
          # non-zero here and fails the job, which is correct and means the
          # backstop below is skipped -- it exists for a run that exits 0.
          python3 tools/precedent_check.py 2>&1 | tee check-output.txt

      - name: Refuse a run that checked nothing
        run: |
          set -eu
          # TWO questions, and the earlier version asked only the second. It ran
          # `grep -qE 'precedent_check: 0 passed'`, which is silent when the
          # summary line never appeared at all -- an engine that dies after its
          # per-check lines but still exits 0, a truncated pipe, or a future
          # rewording. Measured with a stub printing per-check lines and no
          # summary: the workflow went green having verified nothing, through
          # the very refusal meant to be its backstop.
          #
          # Anchored at ^ so a per-check line, or this message quoted in the
          # output, cannot be mistaken for the summary.
          summary="$(grep -E '^precedent_check: [0-9]+ passed' check-output.txt | tail -1 || true)"
          if [ -z "$summary" ]; then
            echo "No precedent_check summary line in the output. The run exited 0" \
                 "without reporting what it checked, so nothing here can be called" \
                 "verified. The captured output is above." >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          passed="$(printf '%s' "$summary" | sed -E 's/^precedent_check: ([0-9]+) passed.*/\1/')"
          if [ "$passed" -eq 0 ]; then
            echo "Refusing a run that passed ZERO checks. Every check either skipped" \
                 "or did not run, and a skip is not a pass -- a green result here" \
                 "would mean nothing was verified. Summary line: $summary" >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          echo "$summary"

  views-drift:
    name: Generated views match a fresh regeneration
    needs: debounce
    if: needs.debounce.outputs.skip != 'true'
    runs-on: ${{ vars.PRECEDENT_RUNNER || 'ubuntu-latest' }}

    # WHY THIS CHECK EXISTS (practice: cite-the-incident). Every set that
    # vendors the engine generates AGENTS.md's loader block, MAP.md and
    # GLOSSARY.md -- and until 2026-09-11 nothing checked any of them
    # anywhere but in BestPractice itself, where deep-check.yml runs
    # verify_harness.py. verify_harness.py is deliberately not vendored into
    # a source set (precedent_vendor_engine.py says so in as many words), so
    # the guard existed in exactly one repo out of all of them.
    #
    # What that cost, measured: an individual set's MAP.md sat three
    # practices stale -- claiming 12 practice files while practices/ held
    # 15 -- under a generated header saying a check was failing the build on
    # exactly that. Nothing was failing. Nobody looked.
    #
    # PARTIALLY, BUT NOT WHOLLY, COVERED BY THE precedent-check JOB ABOVE.
    # precedent_check.py's own generated-artifact-provenance check (binds
    # this repo since BestPractice PR #261) runs build_views.py --check as a
    # subprocess too, but only for MAP.md and GLOSSARY.md -- whole generated
    # files, checked by their own stamp. AGENTS.md's generated LOADER BLOCK
    # is a different shape (a hand-authored file with one generated
    # section), and its own drift check lives in verify_harness.py, which is
    # deliberately not vendored here. So this job is the ONLY thing in this
    # repo that catches loader-block drift -- removing it would reopen
    # exactly the incident above for the one view generated-artifact-
    # provenance cannot see. Kept as its own job, not folded into the
    # precedent-check job, so a loader-block drift keeps this check's own
    # clear failure message instead of surfacing as a generic registry
    # violation.
    #
    # IT GATES, IT DOES NOT FIX. Regenerating in CI would leave the branch's
    # own diff wrong and put a runner bot in the authorship path that
    # ci-commits-carry-identity exists to keep clean. The fix is one
    # `python3 tools/build_views.py` on the branch, by whoever drifted it.
    steps:
      - name: Check out the pull request head
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}
          # fetch-depth left at the default: nothing here walks history.

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"

      - name: Decide what this repo generates
        id: layout
        run: |
          set -eu
          if [ ! -f tools/build_views.py ]; then
            if [ -f process/upstream/tools/build_views.py ]; then
              echo "This repo vendors the engine under process/upstream/, which is" \
                   "INSTALL.md section 1's CONSUMING-repo layout: its practices/ is" \
                   "materialized from sources that do not exist in a CI checkout, so" \
                   "there is nothing here to check the generated views against. A run" \
                   "that passed would be reporting coverage it does not have. Remove" \
                   "this workflow; GITHUB_ACTIONS.md's Limits section says what does" \
                   "cover a consuming repo." >&2
              exit 1
            fi
            echo "No tools/build_views.py -- the engine was never vendored into this" \
                 "repo, or it lives somewhere this workflow does not know about." \
                 "Vendor it (tools/precedent_vendor_engine.py) or add the path here." >&2
            exit 1
          fi

          # A repo that has not instantiated its instructions file yet has no
          # generated views at all, and build_views.py exits non-zero saying
          # so. That is a real message for a person and a wrong one for a
          # gate: nothing has drifted, because nothing is generated. A
          # brand-new bootstrapped set is in exactly this state, and a red
          # check on its very first pull request is an adopter's first
          # impression of the whole system. Report NOT CHECKED in those
          # words rather than passing quietly.
          if ! grep -q 'BEGIN GENERATED: precedent-loader' AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md 2>/dev/null \
             && ! grep -q 'build_views.py' MAP.md GLOSSARY.md 2>/dev/null; then
            echo "views=none" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
            echo "NOT CHECKED: no generated views in this repo yet -- no loader block" \
                 "in an instructions file, and no MAP.md/GLOSSARY.md stamped by" \
                 "build_views.py. Nothing has drifted because nothing is generated." \
                 "Instantiate the loader block (INSTALL.md) and run" \
                 "python3 tools/build_views.py; this check starts covering them."
            exit 0
          fi

          # MAP.md and GLOSSARY.md are whole generated files in a source
          # set and HAND-AUTHORED from templates/ in a consuming repo, which
          # is the same distinction precedent_check.py's
          # generated-artifact-provenance reads the same way: off the files
          # themselves, by whether they name their generator. Checking a
          # hand-authored MAP.md against a regeneration reports drift for a
          # file that was never generated and never should be.
          if grep -q 'build_views.py' MAP.md GLOSSARY.md 2>/dev/null; then
            echo "views=generated" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          else
            echo "views=agents-only" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          fi

      # --repo . is load-bearing: build_views.py derives its root from its
      # own file location, which is right for a source set and wrong the
      # moment the engine is nested.
      - name: Check the generated views
        if: steps.layout.outputs.views != 'none'
        run: |
          set -eu
          args="--repo . --check"
          if [ "${{ steps.layout.outputs.views }}" = "agents-only" ]; then
            args="$args --agents-only"
          fi
          # build_views.py exits 0 when a declared source is unreachable --
          # "not verified is not a failure, and not a pass either", in its
          # own words. For a gate it has to be a failure: the whole job of
          # this check is to be something that actually looked.
          set +e
          out="$(python3 tools/build_views.py $args 2>&1)"
          status=$?
          set -e
          printf '%s\n' "$out"
          if [ "$status" -ne 0 ]; then
            exit "$status"
          fi
          if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q 'NOT VERIFIABLE'; then
            echo "This repo's loader block is built from sources a CI runner cannot" \
                 "reach, so this workflow cannot gate it -- see the NOTICE lines" \
                 "above for which source and why. Run" \
                 "'python3 tools/precedent_sync_views.py --repo . --check' in a" \
                 "session where every declared source resolves, and remove this" \
                 "workflow from this repo: a green check here would mean nothing." >&2
            exit 1
          fi
